          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux, or gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), often occurs after meals for several reasons. One primary cause is that certain foods or large meal sizes can trigger symptoms by putting extra pressure on the stomach and lower esophageal sphincter. This pressure can force stomach contents, including acid, back into the esophagus. \n\nFatty meals, for instance, slow down the emptying of the stomach, extending the time during which reflux can happen. In addition, eating large or late meals, as well as drinking alcohol or carbonated beverages, can relax the valve that keeps stomach acid from moving upward, which may enhance the likelihood of reflux occurring after eating (source: \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)\" and \"Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers\"). \n\nPeople may notice more severe symptoms when bending forward or lying down shortly after eating, as these positions can also increase pressure on the area where the esophagus meets the stomach (source: \"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)\"). \n\nIf you have persistent symptoms, it would be advisable to discuss them with a healthcare professional to explore management options tailored for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
